Windows: Let the host say for which calls a thread leaves hardware TSO.

On macOS a thread is switched to hardware TSO when it enters JIT code and off
again before native code handles a system call or a unix call, because native
code is slower under TSO. Each switch is a Mach trap, so every such call costs
two.

Measured on an M4 Pro (macos/tests/cpu-bench.sh), from x86_64 code, with the
switch and with software TSO, which has none:
  QueryPerformanceCounter         220 ns   21 ns   (native arm64: 16 ns)
  WaitForSingleObject(event, 0)   246 ns   47 ns
  SetEvent + ResetEvent           445 ns   43 ns
A game makes such calls, and Vulkan calls, which are unix calls, many
thousands of times a frame. What staying on costs native code
(macos/tests/native_tso.c): a bulk fill takes twice as long, a bulk move 10 to
25% longer, reading and locking the same.

FEX_HARDWARE_TSO_OFF chooses: "all" (as before, also when unset), "syscall",
"unix" or "none". The unix library reads it and returns it with the probe; the
ARM64EC module installs only the dispatcher wrappers that are wanted, and the
WOW64 module switches off only for the calls that are wanted. A thread that
stays on needs no trap at its next entry into JIT code. FEX's log line says
which it is.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR FEX. Applied by macos/build-fex.sh to
github.com/athei/FEX at 8425ea974e072821c75969723efe49cca605200c. Written
with an AI assistant (Claude). FEX takes no AI-generated code contributions
(its CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be
sent to it as a pull request. What was found may be reported to its
developers, for them to write their own change.
